A||||13||MrFieroUK||Improved Front Brakes||||14||
Z||000000||MrFieroUK||12-14-2001||09:36 AM||||The last time this issue was discussed was in this thread;<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/015540.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/015540.html</A> <P>The point of interest was highlighted from a discussion in Theogres cave concerning swapping primary and secondary lines from the master cylinder to the balance block/proportioning valve assembly.<P>The problem that arose, centered around GM's insistance on using two different sized lines from each chamber in the master cylinder.<P>In the last few days I've been able to obtain and install a reducer union assembly on each line to facilitate the swap.<P>As a forenote, the GA calipers/rotors/Blazer M/C have had 500+ miles to settle in and on road, feel was certainly improving. The issue now rested on whether swapping the primary & secondary master cylinder lines around would improve initial response to the pedal. The primary piston has a quick take up section which would more quickly deal with the "dead travel" in the pedal caused by the strong piston pull back present in the GA calipers (but not in the OE Fiero calipers.)<P>From stock, I fabricated 4 lines, 2 of 1/4" O/D and two of 3/16" O/D, coupling one of each with a reducer union assembly to facilitate the crossover. You can see the finished result from the picture below.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.teslayn-eng.demon.co.uk/Fiero%20brake%20line%20swap.JPG"> <P>Note that the secondary (1/4") pipe now goes to the balance block union nearest the servo, and vice-versa for the primary.<P>Each reducer union assembly (two required) was made up of (left to right);<P>1.) A female 7/16" UNF/Whitworth for the 1/4" pipe,<BR>2.) An inline coupler with  7/16" 20UNF male one one end and a 3/8" 24 UNF female on the other,<BR>3.) A male 3/8" 24 UNF male for the 3/16" pipe.<P>All parts made from Brass - see the picture below;<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.teslayn-eng.demon.co.uk/brake%20union%20assy.JPG"> <P>Was the effort worth it? Yes. The "dead travel" is effectively gone, i.e. you are aware of retardation almost immediately the pedal is depressed. It is much easier to modulate brake pressure right through to front wheel lock up with out needing the muscles of a champion marathon runner. So overall control and feel is much improved, even on slippery surfaces, as I can attest to this morning on a 160 mile trip to London where heavy traffic and black ice do not a friendly environment make! On a busy urban freeway, on a slippery/icey surface, I was able to avoid rear ending a vehicle which stopped suddenly in front of me because of the better control and modulation.<P>Now what remains is the need to sort out these infernal rear brakes and e-brake mechanism. The much improved front brakes show up the poor rears even more. Although there is no indication that the rears are any less effective than they were before, I am still aware of the car attempting to, as it were "overrun" the front of the car and heave its mass forward under firm braking conditions, especially when the brakes are stone cold.<P>In that previous thread, I mentioned a similar problem with my 1997 Chrysler Neon (35,000 miles from new). As with the Fiero the problem was particularl;y noticeable from cold. Last week, I did a routine service on the car and inspected the rear pads with the intention of replacing them if necessary. The wear showed it was about time to change with around 4 to 5 thousand miles left on the better outboard pads, and the inners on the wear limit. I noticed how heavily extended the pistons were. The pistons are relatively small in diameter and were pushed out a distance of about 65% to 70% of their diameter. They looked "unstable"and unable to contribute 100% of their braking efficiency. Certainly, with new pads, the cold braking performance is very much improved.<P>This leads me to believe that, clearly (as before) the Fiero's rears are not doing anywhere near enough work. When you consider that a front wheel driven car has more braking force apportioned to rear brakes - even though the majority of its mass is over the front wheels - then given the slight rearward bias of mass on the Fiero, it is clear that the rear brakes need to do a lot more work in order to preserve overall control and feel.<P><BR>I will attempt to have the braking efficiency of all 4 corners checked in the next week or so - as a comparison with pre-modification performance with rebuilt OEM brakes - before moving to the next stage.<P>The OEM rears brakes should be more than adequate with increased line pressure. However, the E-brake is a next to useless design on these calipers - no way can you attempt hand brake turns - and a revised set up with different calipers, e-brake and vented rotors is on the cards. More anon...<P>The information in this posting is not intended as a definitive and guaranteed method of improving your brakes. It represents the cumulated experience of the author, and is intended as a guide only. Anyone modifying their car in like manner does so entirely at their own risk. BRAKES ARE IN IMPORTANT SAFETY DEVICE. THEY SHOULD NOT BE ALTERED BY ANYONE INEXPERIENCED IN BRAKE OVERHAUL METHODS OR UNSURE OF WHAT IS INVOLVED. LEAVE SUCH WORK TO A SKILLED TECHNICIAN.||||reg||14
Z||000001||VISCERAL||12-14-2001||02:24 PM||||Slammed Fiero and I removed the brake proportioning spring in the aluminum body to increase the rear bias on our 11.25" brakes all around. works well, less lock up on the front, greater bear brake usage, no rear lockups. This was an autocrosser's original suggestion, maybe you might want to try it. Its the spring in the valve body facing the rear of the car. Just remove it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||12-14-2001||07:53 PM||||I'm glad it's working out so far. If anyone else has tried it, they haven't said how it worked. So far what you've reported is very much what I expected.<P>I'm a bit worried that you used brass. I'd recomend changing to steel ASAP. Brass is known to stress crack when pounded by the pressures in a brake line. There's no way to tell how long it will last. Remember, you're dealing with upwards of 1000PSI at full line pressure. Brass should probably be considered a temporay test fitting.<P>They make steel adaptors so that one size nut/line will fit another size hole in the MC or whatever w/o splicing. they can be hard to find. I've used them when I had to replace a line but couldn't duplicate one or more ends.<P>I especially liked that last paragiraf (spellin intentional).<P>I'm not sure where you came to the conclusion that the rear brakes get more bias in FWD.... <P>Brake bias in OE setups can run any where from about 60/40 to 90/10 (F/R) bias. FWD cars tend toward the latter.<P>Bias setup is dependant mainly on 2 things... Weight transfer and transmission. The actual car weight distribution isn't as large a factor as how that weight shifts durring stopping. A rear heavy vehicle can put way more load up front than you might think. Suspension matters too... if the suspension is sloppy then you'll have a more rapid weight shift.<P>Weight transfer is the biggest function that decides it but transmission matter because an Automatic provides constant engine loads to the wheels. <P>In a stick RWD car, the rears need less power. Obviously the clutch is pushed in allot of the time, disconnecting engine load... You've also got to count in much more engine braking. Too much rear caliper combined with engine brake effect can start a spin really easy. This is why in many cars you have differant rear brake parts on stick and auto modles. The parts may not be differant at the axle but prop valves are often setup differantly.<P>Do you know which pistons are in your calipers? if they are the plug face pistons then they may have internal problems screwing up the hand brake even more than normal.<P>If you go to another caliper in rear, worry about a good e brake setup more than the vented rotor. <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||artherd||12-15-2001||12:51 AM|||| <A HREF="http://www.heldmotorsports.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.heldmotorsports.com</A> <P>The 12/13" kits will solve ALL your problems  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Really not all that expensive either.<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000004||MrFieroUK||12-15-2001||06:14 AM||||Theogre, brass fittings were supplied by a UK brake specialist company with parts to the appropriate British Standard. I would say these items are not made from the usual(?)typical brittle brass alloy, but a more ductile compound which is not prone to the type of stress fracture you refer to. In fact I had to check in their catalogue for material type, because they just didn't "feel" like regular brass fittings. Initially I thought they were brass plated steel!<P>When you refer to plug face pistons, what exactly do you mean? The pistons on this car have four machined round recesses at the outer diameter for locating a windback tool and the corresponding lugs on the brake pads.<P>I agree that the e-brake is critical. Anyway in the UK we have no choice in the matter. Parking brakes must attain a minimum of 16% force relative to the vehicle's GVWR. In homologation testing of American vehicles for the UK market, I'm seeing typically 35% to 45% efficiencies whether tested statically or dynamically. Frankly, this setup is disappointing, coming from a major manufacturer like GM. Far from being an EMERGENCY, brake it hardly qualifies as a parking brake! The hydraulic pressure to the rears has to overcaome, not only spring pressure in the prop. valve, but also the seal friction and spring pressure in the rear caliper piston mechanism. So, as you say, if the e-brake system is faulty or out of adjustment in any way, then rear service brake efficiency will be impaired. When I serviced the rear calipers, the piston e-brake mech. was stripped and sorted also. It was interesting to note I had to replace the internal piston/mech. lip seal with appropriate O-rings, because the originals had corroded to the inside of the piston wall! Just a plain bad design.<P>I am using the brake standards for ECWVTA (European Community Whole Vehicle Type Approval) as a guide to setting the performance parameters for this car's brakes. The most arduous part of that specification is the depletion test - one that many small, lightweight cars find hard to achieve. It requires that a passenger vehicle's total braking force with a depleted servo (engine off - servo pumped out) achieves 30% under an applied pedal pressure of 50 Kg (about 480 Newtons)maximum.<P>The implications are that a servoed system would need to be able to achieve lock up on a roller brake test. The Fiero rears don't come anywhere near that!<P>As an example, my 1989 Lincoln LSC had its annual inspection last week. It weighs in at about 900 lbs more than the Fiero (Kerb weight) at lock up the rears acheived 350 Kgs a piece (service brake) and 240Kgs a piece on the parking brake (disc type - no lock-up.) So, without allowing for the weight/configuration differences between the Fiero and Lincoln, that's about a 300%+ difference (Fiero acheived 100Kg on service and e-brake at the last test 5 months ago, and didn't achieve lock-up.)<P>Artherd thanks for the tip, but I don't want to change wheels - I like the original appearance. In any case, 9.75" rotors should be more than enough for a car of this weight, irrespective of drive train. Afterall, no matter how fast you accelerate or how high your terminal velocity (within sane limits) you still have a similar mass to decelerate - 4 or 8 cylinder - it doesn't matter!||||reg||5
Z||000005||MrFieroUK||12-15-2001||11:13 AM||||On the matter of front wheel drive brake bias, I'll admit my knowledge is limited to mainly midsize and compact vehicles (vans et al.) However, those which I have homologated for the UK show SIGNIFICANTLY more rear bias than the Fiero demonstrates. Unless I've missed something fundemental (always possible) when rebuilding the Fiero stock rears, then this is just one bad design, pure and simple, whether or not the bias was intentionally set this way.||||reg||13
Z||000006||theogre||12-15-2001||01:18 PM||||I very much apreciate the testing you are doing and the reporting of it. I think you are the only one so far to test at this advanced a level. <P>The brake dyno you use sounds differant from the one I'm used to. Here in Delaware you drive onto a skid plate type thing. It's divided into 4 areas. when you stop on it it sends measurements to the computer. In this type of test, you get a very close view of how the car stops on the road.<P>If I understand your machine... the car is on a roller set more like a chassis dyno. It has to stop the drums within specified force. Such a test is good for functional work but I have to wonder how relevant it is for stopping performance if they eliminate the weight transfer from the picture. Are there any government sites you can find that explain these machines and their use? I'm at a loss for a starting place on them.<P>Brass.... as long as they are made for the job then they shouldn't be a problem. Most people using brass here would use standard plumbers brass, which is a big problem.<P>The piston design is covered in my cave... We also covered it more in this thread, <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/014749.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/014749.html</A>  Bruce at FTF has had all the Fiero rear pistons apart. He can explain the exact failures better. The main problem as I understand it is the ball bearing set inside the piston. The bearing goes to hell then the E brake doesn't work right or at all.<P>Yes, if the hand brake parts inside the caliper are bad, the rear calipers will have problems. Also, if you haven't already, check the total pad cearance using the manual adjustment method in my cave. That can have a big impact on rear brake performance. I was really surprised how much differance it made when I did that adjustment on my 87SC. If the pads have excessive clearance it will effect both service and hand brake. It can kill the hand brake completely.<P>The plug faced pistons are the worse ones. These litterally have a silicon rubber plug in the piston face just like in my cave illustration.<P>I'll admit I'm not very up on EU/UK car standards. I have spent allot of time reading in US standards. You can get at them online from the safety links in my cave. (Which will change to a much longer links page in the next day or 3) the server that generates the acrobat files can be slow. try it at night or early morning US eastern time. (The acrobat ones include any illustrations in the original text.)<P>While the hand brake will stop the car some time just before hell freezes, It's mainly a parking brake. The test for that is in FMVSS standard 135. In summary, you park the car nose down a specified grade and the brake has to keep it still for at least X minutes. park the car nose up the same grade and test again. If there's a test for stopping distance with it, I didn't see it. It may be mixed in with other tests. The FMVSS standards are a tought read even for an Ogre....<P>As a first test, I think I'd yank out the prop valve spring/guts just to eliminate it from the equasion. This will give you max rear line pressure. I think Fiero rear line pressure is regulated to about 600-700PSI max. might even be less knowing GM. They were terrified of a repeat of the Corvair problems made famous a few decades ago. If need be you can recalibrate the valve to meet your standards later.<P>If you need to look at the math of the system more, there is an Excel spread sheet in my cave that has most of the numbers in it. That should let you work out the the bias at full line pressure. (Ignoring the caliper spring issue.) This info whould help you sort out what numbers the machine shows and give you an idea how much if any pressure the springs eat in the rear pistons.<P>As I understand them, the 2 springs in the rear caliper are doing a balancing act. The springs are stiff, but I beleive the differance in stiffness between them is the factor you'd be after. In a caliper that is working right I don't think they should eat much pressure. In a caliper that's messed up or has too much pad clearance they could waste quite a bit of pressure.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||MrFieroUK||12-15-2001||02:14 PM||||Thanks Ogre. I'll do some more swatting up on the links you supplied.<P>To answer your question, yes, in the UK the roller brake test is done one axle at a time, on something like a chassis dyno, rather than a dynamic drive-on pressure table such as you've described.<P>However, for longitudinal brake balance (distribution ratio - used only during initial pre-registration approvals) deleted servo brake force readings are done at different pedal pressures for each axle. The results are then processed by an equation. The rollers also contain an acurate set of scales to determine the mass borne by each axle;<P>Total Rear Axle Brake Effort (f2)/Total Front Axle Brake Effort (f1) = Braking ratio at 'X' pedal effort.<P>Front Axle Maximum Tyre/Road Friction Force<P>F1 =(W1+(Wt x H/E x (f1+f2)/Wt))(f1+f2)/Wt<P>Rear Axle Maximum Tyre/Road Friction Force<P>F2 =(W1-(Wt x H/E x (f1+f2)/Wt))(f1+f2)/Wt<P>Where W = weight in "running order" (curb weight with a full tank of gas), H = centre of gravity, E = wheelbase, F = brake effort, 1,2 or t = axles.<BR>Friction force ratio = F2/F1<P>This calculation is done for each of the required brake pedal force applications of 'X'. The braking ratio must be less than the friction force ratio for all values of total brake force.<P>Fun, isn't it??<P>As a side bar: I've not fully studied all of FMVSS 105 and 135 (I have a copy of CFR49, October 1996 edition), but shouldn't we be careful in accessing the Fiero's brake performance against 135? FMVSS 135 only became mandatory in September 2000 and voluntary from September 1996, prior to that all vehicles (hydraulic systems, at least) were built to FMVSS 105 as are a lot of current model truck based SUVs. I don't know how much of 105 transferred over to 135.<P>Interestingly, 135 is the only Federal VSS to be considered equivalent to the corresponding EU ECE standards. Amazing isn't it, that in some 50 years of the World Trade Organisation's Working Party 29, that's all that our global lords and masters have been able to agree on where vehicle safety standards are concerned?! Truth is, the standards are so similar that what remains is a case of nit-picking. <P>Methinks power brokering is more important than passenger safety! As an example, we all know that balance blocks have a bypass passage to allow full hydraulic pressure to be applied to the rears in event of a failure on the front brakes. This new homologation test in the UK will not accept vehicles (without minimum rear ABS) on the basis of their manufacturer's design for longitudinal braking performance. Instead, as quite a few very light sub-compacts and a few mini-vans (we call them people carriers) mostly from Japan etc. have too high a rear brake performance, then owners are required to fit a proportioning valve in the rear brake line. Now, if they have a failure on the fronts their remaining brake power will be severely reduced. Vehicles with a short wheelbase/long overhangs and high C of G come off worse.<P>I'll keep the forum apprised of ongoing results. I very much appreciate everyone's input and time. It is by such an exchange that we can all benefit and keep our cherished classic on the road in an increasingly improved form to cope with modern traffic.||||reg||4
Z||000008||MrFieroUK||12-15-2001||02:23 PM||||Ogre, For info on brake roller testers used in EU check out this link<BR> <A HREF="http://www.maha.de/englisch/Home-pro.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.maha.de/englisch/Home-pro.htm</A> ||||reg||14
Z||000009||theogre||12-15-2001||04:49 PM||||I'll have to see more about FMVSS 105. I can't remember which one says what without both in front of me. I'll also look and see if I can find the ones that were current for the fiero build periods. I don't think they go back that far online. My understanding is that the car is built to meet whatever was in effect at the time but that all modifications must meet the current stricter standards. It's hard to get clear info from NHTSA.<P>That roller thing is scarry. I'm glad I don't have to mess with that.... <P>I came to the conclusion a long time ago that safety "standards" are more about lip service than anything else. U.S. car makers have fought every single standard on the books. The standards are more often a balance of what the car insurance companies want vs. what the car makers will tolerate. It's already well proven cars can be made far safer than they are. Even then we end up with things like Ford Explorers and their tire problems. That problem has been going on for years.... but I digress too much.<P>If the car wasn't built to even the lame test in 135, then it's a wonder the hand brake does anything at all.... <P>The thing about the added prop valve is scary too.... That's just amazing....<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||MrFieroUK||12-15-2001||07:29 PM||||Ogre,<BR>According to my edition of CFR 49, FMVSS105 was first made effective by Federal Regulation 29696, July 19, 1976 and was updated several dozen times - the last listed as an update to section 6.9.1, S6.9.2, S6.10.1 and S6.10.2 effective March 1, 1999.<P>I find it amazing that FMVSS's can be made retrospective, relative to the vehicles age! I could understand, for example, if a younger engine was swapped in that the later emission standard should apply.<P>Don't be scared by the brake test rollers - the vehicle's wheels are driven by the brake tester at a couple of miles per hour - not vice versa!<P>Do be scared about the additional proportioning valve nonsense! I've been involved in discussions with and actions against the UK Min. of Transport Vehicle Safety and Engineering department for 5 years since this homologation test for non-European type approved imported vehicles was first mooted. It has been a real Battle Royale! Some of the nonsense that they've spouted has defied all logic and common sense. At one point, during the pilot study period, it looked as if no more new American vehicles (up to ten years of age) would ever get into the UK again!!! As we found out during our fights/meetings/discussions/legal actions - it was a piece of technical racism on the part of the car manufacturers and their franchised dealers to slam the door on ANYTHING not approved and sold by them. The fights go on. My last meeting with civil servants in VSE was just a couple of weeks ago, when I had the privilege of pointing out their technical folly yet again.<P>Would you believe these cars have to be put on a set of rollers - similar to the brake tester, but with the provision for permenant 4WD - that are driven by the vehile's drive train, to check the speedo calibration at up to and including ---- get this ----SEVENTY MILES PER HOUR. I kid you not... there have been 2 driven roller bearing failures resulting in seizure at two different testing stations within the last three months... both occured at max. speed.<P>In fact when I'm at the testing station next week with an '02 Bullit Mustang, I'll take a 'photo or two and post them up for all to see... you will not believe your eyes!<P>I could write a book - in fact, several volumes - about our experiences over the last 5 years...||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||12-15-2001||11:09 PM||||All I can say to that one is,,, Holly $hit. And I thought USDOT/NHTSA were glue sniffers... ||||reg||12
Z||000012||lowCG||12-16-2001||01:27 AM||||Ogre,I believe you nailed it when you mentioned the Corvair fear-thing,my car has yet to break the rear wheels loose under hard braking with the proportioning spring removed.<BR>I do have some 11" brakes in front,but I'd thought there'd be more back there than that!||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||12-22-2001||02:53 PM||||MrFieroUK,,,<P>I was reading thru the 2000 571.105 and 571.135 to compare the tests for the hand brake. Both standards are virtually identical for the parking brake system. Neither specification appears to contain a stopping test for this system. Instead they specify only a static holding test. (I haven't been able to review older doc's yet. I'm hoping to find someplace I can read a mid 80's version. NARA only goes back to early 90's online.)<P>The specifications for both static tests appear the same. To summarize... <P>With the transmission in neutral, the hand brake must hold the car in place on a 20% grade (18 degree slope) for 5 minutes. The car is tested nose up and nose down the grade. <P>Standard 571.105 has an additional test for automatic transmission. In that test, with the transmission in park, the brake must hold the car on a 30% grade for 5 minutes. The car is again tested nose up and nose down the grade.<P>The complete lack of a stopping test in either standard explains much. Specifically, it explains how a carmaker can get away with the performance level of a typical Fiero handbrake system.<P>As far as I can tell... This is all the testing that has ever been required on the parking brake system. 571.105 looks like it’s older than dirt. It appears to be the standard for all cars built since the FMVSS was developed. That or it’s a relatively minor update to the original standard. Even if built today, the Fiero’s lame hand brake can likely meet the test in both standards, at least when it works.<P>From a reliability standpoint, you want rear calipers that Do Not have the original style pistons with a silicone plug in the face. That piston type has known internal defects that will affect both the hand and service brake functions. To get rid of them, the caliper needs to be rebuilt using the new solid faced piston with its matching spring and screw hardware. The GM kit for this is # 18019027 if it’s still available.<P>I would not recommend doing any further work on EU/UK certification until you verify the rear pistons are operational. If everything else in the hand brake system were good, I’d suspect a piston is bad. I’m debating rebuilding/replacing one of my calipers simply because it still has an old style piston. The old style pistons are subjects of one recall. (It’s listed in the recall notes in my cave.) While my car is an automatic, I do use my hand brake rather often when it works.||||reg||1
